#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723 :topic/difficulty 0.64807000989903 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428431 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1904->149. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120} :encompassing/weight 1.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428432 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1); linear=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1904->2083. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980} :encompassing/weight 0.3333333333333333}] :topic/id #uuid "0e15397e-6c32-508c-b6ea-90f1a9e5a237" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124477 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124290 :question/id #uuid "ccfdc303-534f-530e-810b-f475a997f5d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4703" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its pivot columns?\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124291 :question/id #uuid "22a77802-4114-516f-a0e0-4cda2e069361" :question/math-academy-id "e-4704" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $C$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124479 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124292 :question/id #uuid "6e4b2e85-86a8-5050-a60a-ae8d40453ad0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4705" :question/problem "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1073953e-7e0a-5237-8533-8882b7772741" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Pivot Columns of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124480 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124293 :question/id #uuid "3ac076bd-5add-5c08-bb96-7b11c0a37d54" :question/math-academy-id "e-4706" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{7}\\right){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1904 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :topic/title "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}]}
